<v Lina Misitzis>The next year, 1999, the divorce. Porn splintered off from CES. AVN began hosting their own expo only for adult content. And for a while, they still held it the same weekend as CES. CES, meanwhile, banned porn from their show, though, obviously, some sex stuff still found its way onto the show floor.
<v Lina Misitzis>第二年，1999 年，离婚了。色情内容从 CES 中分离出来。AVN 开始举办他们自己的博览会，只针对成人内容。有一段时间，他们仍然在 CES 的同一个周末举办。与此同时，CES 禁止了色情内容进入他们的展会，尽管显然，一些性产品还是设法进入了展厅。

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:51.750
<v Lina Misitzis>Which brings us back to 2019. With her tech award gone and her sex toy robot banned from CES, Lora wrote another letter, this one an open letter, and posted it to Osé's website on midnight, January 8, the day CES was starting in Vegas.
<v Lina Misitzis>这把我们带回了 2019 年。随着她的科技奖项被撤销，她的性玩具机器人被 CES 禁止，萝拉写了另一封信，这是一封公开信，并于 1 月 8 日午夜，即 CES 在拉斯维加斯开始的那天，发布在 Osé 的网站上。

00:32:51.750 --> 00:33:01.890
<v Lora Haddock>Our very first bit of coverage surfaced right about 9:00 AM. And all of a sudden, the floodgates opened.
<v Lora Haddock>我们的第一篇报道在大约早上 9 点出现。突然间，洪水闸门打开了。

00:33:01.890 --> 00:33:20.460
<v Lina Misitzis>The story was picked up by Fast Company, The New York Times, Fortune, Gizmodo, Glamour, Cosmo, Wired, The Guardian, and more. Headlines like "Women's Sexuality is Still Taboo for Tech" were popping up, and "Sex Toy Debacle Reveals Shameful Double Standard at CES."
<v Lina Misitzis>这个故事被《Fast Company》、《纽约时报》、《财富》、《Gizmodo》、《魅力》、《Cosmo》、《连线》、《卫报》等媒体采用。像“女性性需求对科技界依然是禁忌”这样的标题随处可见，还有“性玩具惨剧揭示了 CES 可耻的双重标准”。

00:33:20.460 --> 00:33:26.640
<v Lina Misitzis>A month later, in February, Lora gets an email from the assistant to CTA President Gary Shapiro.
<v Lina Misitzis>一个月后，在 2 月份，萝拉收到了 CTA 主席加里·夏皮罗助理发来的一封电子邮件。

00:33:26.640 --> 00:34:00.030
<v Lora Haddock>He says Gary Shapiro would like to talk to you. Can we schedule a phone call? And I mean, at that point, I'm nervous. Like, what does he want to talk to me about? OK. We get on the call, and the first thing Gary says is I owe you all a very big apology. We did you a great disservice, and we're very sorry for what's happened. And we realize that we have made a mistake.
<v Lora Haddock>他说加里·夏皮罗想和你谈谈。我们可以预约一个电话吗？我的意思是，在那一点上，我很紧张。比如，他想跟我谈什么？好的。我们通话了，加里说的第一件事是我欠你们大家一个大大的道歉。我们对你们造成了极大的伤害，对发生的一切感到非常抱歉。我们意识到我们犯了一个错误。

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:08.140
<v Lina Misitzis>Lora and her team were being given their award back.
<v Lina Misitzis>萝拉和她的团队拿回了他们的奖项。

00:34:08.140 --> 00:34:33.920
<v Lina Misitzis>At the end of the press release came a promise of new and updated policies. They'd make CES a more, quote, "welcoming and inclusive event for all." The first new policy was that they'd include sex products in the CES show on a one year trial basis. It was not clear why they called this a new policy since sex products were actually in the show the previous year. They said these products would now be in the category Health and Wellness.
<v Lina Misitzis>新闻稿的最后是一个关于新政策和更新政策的承诺。他们会让 CES 成为一个更具包容性的活动，引用，“欢迎所有人”。第一个新政策是他们将在为期一年的试行基础上，将性产品纳入 CES 展会。不清楚为什么他们称之为新政策，因为性产品实际上在去年就出现在展会上了。他们说这些产品现在将被归入“健康与保健”类别。

00:34:33.920 --> 00:35:10.530
<v Lina Misitzis>And they rolled out one other new policy to address something else they'd gotten a lot of criticism for in the past. They instituted a dress code. Quote, "Booth personnel may not wear clothing that is sexually revealing or clothing that reveals an excess of bare skin or body conforming clothing that hugs genitalia." What CES was trying to address was booth babes. Booth babes and CES have always gone together since the 1960s. These are models in revealing clothing, or body conforming clothing that hugs genitalia, hired to stand next to the tech on the show floor.
<v Lina Misitzis>他们还推出了另一项新政策，以解决他们过去受到很多批评的另一个问题。他们制定了着装规定。引用，“展位人员不得穿着露骨的服装，或露出过多裸露皮肤的服装，或紧贴生殖器的紧身服装。” CES 试图解决的问题是“展位宝贝”。自从 20 世纪 60 年代以来，展位宝贝和 CES 一直如影随形。这些模特穿着暴露的服装，或者是紧贴生殖器的紧身服装，被雇佣来站在展厅的科技产品旁边。

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:33.260
<v Lina Misitzis>In a 1986 story for the Toronto Star, Jonathan Gross wrote, quote, "The quality of the products was inversely proportional to the chest size of the booth babes handing out the literature." Paul Fishbein sold AVN years ago to pursue a career in mainstream TV. But when I told him about CES's new policies, he wasn't surprised at all.
<v Lina Misitzis>在 1986 年为《多伦多星报》撰写的一篇报道中，乔纳森·格罗斯写道，引用，“产品的质量与分发宣传资料的展位宝贝的胸部大小成反比。” 保罗·费什拜因多年前卖掉了 AVN，去追求主流电视事业。但当我告诉他 CES 的新政策时，他一点也不惊讶。

00:35:33.260 --> 00:35:43.160
<v Paul Fishbein>It's the same thing. It's the 2020 version of everything we just talked about. Nothing has changed. Sex scares them.
<v Paul Fishbein>这是一回事。这是我们刚才谈论的所有内容的 2020 年版本。什么都没变。性让他们感到害怕。

00:35:43.160 --> 00:36:08.810
<v Lina Misitzis>I reached out to CES for comment. They put me on with their executive vice president, Karen Chupka. But there were conditions. CES wouldn't speak with me about the history of their show, the history of sex tech at their show, Lora's product, the award it got, the award being taken away, or the award being given back. All I could ask about was the future of CES. It was a short interview.
<v Lina Misitzis>我联系了 CES 请其置评。他们让我和他们的执行副总裁凯伦·查普卡交谈。但有条件。CES 不会跟我谈论他们展会的历史、他们展会上性科技的历史、萝拉的产品、它获得的奖项、奖项被撤销、或者奖项被给回。我只能问关于 CES 的未来。那是一次简短的采访。

00:36:08.810 --> 00:36:36.170
<v Lina Misitzis>But there was one thing she said that helped shed a little bit of light on their decision making. I asked what would it take for sex to not come back in 2021, after the one year trial? And Karen told me that CES's priority is to make everyone who attends the trade show feel welcome. As long as her 175,000 attendees aren't offended by seeing sex products at the show, sex products can stay. So that's the goal-- satisfying everyone.
<v Lina Misitzis>但她说了一件事，这有助于阐明他们的决策过程。我问，一年试行期后，性产品要怎样才能不回到 2021 年的展会上？凯伦告诉我，CES 的优先事项是让每一位参加贸易展的人都感到受欢迎。只要她的 17 万 5 千名参展者不被在展会上看到性产品所冒犯，性产品就可以留下来。所以这就是目标——满足所有人。

00:36:36.170 --> 00:36:50.540
<v Lina Misitzis>Lora, the inventor, she told me that CES actually consulted with her about what category to put sex products into for this one year trial. CES's instinct? Do it like they used to-- sex in its own category, in its own room.
<v Lina Misitzis>发明者萝拉告诉我，CES 实际上咨询过她，在这个为期一年的试行期内将性产品归入哪个类别。CES 的本能反应是？像以前一样做——性产品自成一类，放在自己的房间里。

00:36:50.540 --> 00:36:58.370
<v Lora Haddock>And I pushed back on that really hard, because I didn't want to be sequestered off in the corner.
<v Lora Haddock>我对此极力反对，因为我不想被隔离在角落里。

00:36:58.370 --> 00:37:05.120
<v Lina Misitzis>So she argued for sex toys to fall under the Health and Wellness category. But me, I don't know if I agree.
<v Lina Misitzis>所以她力争让性玩具归入“健康与保健”类别。但是，我不知道我是否同意。

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:39.400
<v Lina Misitzis>When I think of the sex toys that I have used in my lifetime, I probably can't say on the radio. When I think of, like, the sex toys that I have been aware of in my lifetime, the point, as I understand it, is pleasure. And I think it's OK for products to be marketed to women simply for the fact that they create pleasure. And I actually just-- I wonder why Health and Wellness. I wonder why it couldn't just be in a category called Pleasure. Why isn't pleasure just good enough?
<v Lina Misitzis>当我想到我一生中使用过的性玩具时，我大概不能在广播里说出来。当我想到我一生中接触到的性玩具时，据我理解，重点是愉悦。我觉得产品仅仅因为创造愉悦而向女性营销是可以的。我实际上只是——我纳闷为什么是“健康与保健”。我纳闷为什么不能直接归入一个叫做“愉悦”的类别。为什么“愉悦”本身不够好吗？

00:37:39.400 --> 00:37:45.840
<v Lora Haddock>It is pleasure, but as far as I read it, pleasure is also a part of health and wellness.
<v Lora Haddock>它是愉悦，但据我所知，愉悦也是健康与保健的一部分。

00:37:45.840 --> 00:38:24.850
<v Lina Misitzis>In this one area, Lora's agenda is a lot like CES's. They both want to sell sex. And CES's old solution to the problem was to segregate sex in a separate room in a separate building. But Lora doesn't want to risk losing anyone. She wants to sell to everybody on the main floor with the mainstream products. And if calling her sex robot a health and wellness product gets it out of the lab and into the world, she's fine with that. It's still hidden, just in a different way.
<v Lina Misitzis>在这一领域，萝拉的议程和 CES 的很像。他们都想卖性。而 CES 解决这个问题的旧方案是把性行为隔离在一个单独的建筑里的单独房间里。但萝拉不想冒失去任何人的风险。她想在主流展厅里卖给所有人。如果把她的性机器人称为健康与保健产品能让它走出实验室并推向世界，她没意见。它仍然是隐藏的，只是以另一种方式。

00:38:24.850 --> 00:38:48.160
<v Ira Glass>Lina Misitzis is one of the producers of our show. Coming up, a woman puts on a suit that turns her invisible. What could possibly go wrong in that scenario? That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>莉娜·米西齐斯是我们节目的制作人之一。接下来，一位女性穿上一件让她隐形的套装。在这种情况下可能会出什么错？从芝加哥公共广播电台，一分钟后节目继续。

00:38:48.160 --> 00:39:10.600
<v Ira Glass>This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, Escape from the Lab, stories of the unintended consequences-- yes, unintended consequences-- when a lab subject leaves the lab and heads into the bigger world. We've arrived at Act Three of our show. Act Three, Fraught Couture.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们会选择一个主题，带给你们关于那个主题的不同故事。今天的节目，“逃离实验室”，讲述的是当实验对象离开实验室并前往更大世界时带来的意想不到的后果——是的，意想不到的后果。我们已经到了节目的第三幕。第三幕，“令人担忧的高定”。

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:24.540
<v Ira Glass>So we end our show today with a piece of fiction that was actually written for this episode of our show. In fact, we had this story, and then we went looking for the other stories to put with it. It's by one of our co-workers, Neil Drumming, and it's read for us by actor Susan K. Watson.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们在今天节目的最后是一篇实际上为我们这一集节目撰写的虚构作品。事实上，我们先有了这个故事，然后我们去寻找其他故事来配合它。它由我们的同事尼尔·德拉明撰写，由演员苏珊·K·沃森为我们朗读。

00:39:24.540 --> 00:39:52.550
<v Susan Watson>The clerk at the sporting goods store in Georgetown eyes me like I'm taking a dump in his trail mix. Like a black woman can't purchase camping gear. I toss my new overpriced LL Bean rucksack on the counter with the rest of the stuff, hoping Chet will just ring it up and keep his opinions to himself. Hell, what I really want to do is reach under my collar, power up the special adaptive camouflage on my suit, and poof, disappear right in front of his stupid face.
<v Susan Watson>乔治城那家体育用品店的店员看着我，就像我在他的什锦干果里拉了一泡屎一样。就像一个黑人女性买不起野营装备。我把昂贵的 LL Bean 新背包和剩下的东西扔在柜台上，希望切特能直接结账，把他的偏见留给自己。见鬼，我真正想做的是伸手到领口下面，开启我套装上的特殊自适应伪装，然后砰的一声，在他的蠢脸面前凭空消失。

00:39:52.550 --> 00:40:19.210
<v Susan Watson>But A, I've been a little wary of cloaking since the incident. And B, the suit's in the trunk of my car. I'm all Ann Taylor business casual for my meeting today. And C, Chet and his stank eye are not entirely off base. Truth is, I don't know how to use most of the stuff I'm buying. I was always more of an indoor kid. The whole private military super soldier thing is kind of a fluke.
<v Susan Watson>但 A，自从那次事故以来，我就一直对隐身保持警惕。B，套装在我的车后备箱里。我今天要去开会，穿着安·泰勒的商务休闲装。C，切特和他的那种眼神也不是完全没道理。事实是，我不知道怎么使用我买的大多数东西。我一直是个室内孩子。这种私人军事超级战士的事情纯属侥幸。

00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:35.460
<v Susan Watson>I shove the gear into the trunk of my Honda Civic A2, climb in on the passenger side-- I'm old school-- and recline the seat back as far as it can go. I speak the destination, and the A2 quietly shifts into drive and pulls out.
<v Susan Watson>我把装备塞进我的本田思域 A2 的后备箱，爬进乘客座位——我是老派作风——把座椅调到尽可能往后仰的位置。我说出目的地，A2 悄悄换挡并驶离。

00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:52.440
<v Susan Watson>We've got some time. Let me tell you how I got here. I came out of college doing administrative work for defense subcontractors-- data analysis, asset forfeiture, anything to pay Howard University back and support my impressive weed habit.
<v Susan Watson>我们还有时间。让我告诉你我是怎么到这一步的。大学毕业后我为国防分包商做行政工作——数据分析，资产没收，只要能还上霍华德大学的贷款并支持我令人印象深刻的大麻习惯就行。

00:40:52.440 --> 00:41:29.880
<v Susan Watson>By my early 20s, I had built up a pretty high level of security clearance for a civilian. I was bored behind a desk, though. The first time a field agent let me hold his gun, I was like, yas! I jumped from agency to agency-- DEA, FBI, ICE. And then I pulled a gangster move. Remember Class 8A, the government's now defunct development program for disadvantaged businesses? I launched my own minority led private security company. You know how we do.
<v Susan Watson>20 多岁时，我作为平民已经建立了相当高水平的安全许可。但我坐在桌子后面感到很无聊。第一次有外勤特工让我握他的枪时，我就想，耶！我从一个机构跳槽到另一个机构——缉毒局、联邦调查局、移民及海关执法局。然后我搞了一个黑帮操作。还记得政府现已废除的针对弱势企业的 8A 班吗？我创办了自己的少数族裔主导的私人安全公司。你知道我们怎么做事的。

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:46.650
<v Susan Watson>The world had basically been imploding since 2020, and the US defense tab was wide open. Me and my guys started to piece together a living wage doing Black Ops work-- Latin America, the Mideast, most of it pretty unsavory. Surgical hits, regime change.
<v Susan Watson>世界自 2020 年以来基本上一直在崩溃，美国的国防预算大门敞开。我和我的伙伴们开始通过做黑色行动——拉美、中东，大部分都相当不光彩——来勉强糊口。外科手术式的打击，政权更迭。

00:41:46.650 --> 00:42:11.700
<v Susan Watson>I learned the secret to doing dirt. Do it and walk away. Don't feel anything. I got good at it. But the bigger firms like DynCorp and Sierra kept muscling us aside, snapping up the best jobs in all the global hot zones. That all changed when the suits came out.
<v Susan Watson>我学会了做脏活的秘诀。做了就走。不要有任何感觉。我擅长这个。但像 DynCorp 和 Sierra 这样的大公司一直把我们挤到一边，抢走了全球所有热点地区最棒的工作。当套装问世时，一切都变了。

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:35.940
<v Susan Watson>This was a few years ago. The DoD very discreetly announced that they were looking for subcontractors to field test some new nano engineered adaptive camouflage uniforms. No cool acronym. They were for stealth use in ground conflicts. The suit was made entirely of a melanin based polymer. It was synthetic melanin whipped up in labs.
<v Susan Watson>那是几年前。国防部非常谨慎地宣布，他们正在寻找分包商来实地测试一些新型纳米工程自适应伪装制服。没有酷炫的首字母缩写。它们用于地面冲突中的隐身使用。套装完全由基于黑色素的聚合物制成。那是实验室里合成出来的黑色素。

00:42:35.940 --> 00:43:07.770
<v Susan Watson>But that didn't stop me from raising the fuss in the right circles. I played the race card as hard as I could play it. I mean, the US military was beta testing high tech leotards fashioned from the souls of black folk. Someone in the Defense Department had to have enough PR savvy to understand why they couldn't do that without handing one to the premier intersectional mercenary in the business, me. And so I leveled up.
<v Susan Watson>但这并没有阻止我在正确的圈子里引起轰动。我尽可能狠狠地打出了种族牌。我的意思是，美国军方正在用黑人灵魂构成的面料测试高科技紧身衣。国防部里肯定有人足够精通公关，明白为什么他们不能在不给业内顶尖交叉性雇佣兵——也就是我——一套的情况下做这件事。所以我升级了。

00:43:07.770 --> 00:43:26.550
<v Susan Watson>The A2 pings me as we pull into the parking lot in Pentagon City. That reminds me. I'm going to have to ditch the old girl. I'm playing it too sloppy as it is, running errands in my own car and so close to headquarters. Besides, something tells me autonomous automobiles don't make great getaway cars.
<v Susan Watson>当我们驶入五角大楼城的停车场时，A2 向我发出警报。这提醒了我。我得甩掉那个老姑娘了。我现在跑得太草率了，开着自己的车还离总部这么近。此外，有什么东西告诉我，自动驾驶汽车并不是什么绝佳的逃跑车辆。

00:43:26.550 --> 00:43:47.440
<v Susan Watson>Security waves me through the lobby, staring at my chest in lieu of asking for ID. I'm not going to miss that. Nor will I miss the bland '90s pop cocktail playing in the elevator. Gina's sitting rigidly behind her desk as I walk into her office. I'm not worried. I know why I'm here, why she called me in.
<v Susan Watson>安检人员朝我挥手让我通过大堂，盯着我的胸部而不是要求看身份证。我可不会怀念这个。我也不会怀念电梯里播放的那种平淡的 90 年代流行音乐。我走进她的办公室时，吉娜僵硬地坐在桌子后面。我不担心。我知道我为什么在这里，为什么她把我叫进来。

00:43:47.440 --> 00:44:17.570
<v Susan Watson>She's going to tell me that two weeks ago, there was an incident involving the accidental shooting of a civilian non-combatant in Bamako, Mali. Then she's going to inform me that there's to be an official inquiry into the incident and by which agency. Finally, she's going to lean in, narrow her eyes, and ask me firmly if I had anything to do with said incident. Because she knows that I was in Bamako two weeks ago, shadowing possible members of a radical insurgency.
<v Susan Watson>她会告诉我，两周前，在马里巴马科发生了一起意外枪击平民非战斗人员的事故。然后她会通知我，将对该事故进行正式调查，并由哪个机构进行调查。最后，她会探过身子，眯起眼睛，坚定地问我是否与上述事故有任何关系。因为她知道我两周前就在巴马科，跟踪可能的激进叛乱分子。

00:44:17.570 --> 00:44:48.450
<v Susan Watson>That's her job as my official DoD liaison-- to know where I am at all times, at least as often as I decide to tell her. When Gina and I are off the clock, tangled up in her cozy two-story house in Southeast, I like to call her my handler, then roll out of bed before she can catch me and debrief me. As you were, she yells, before jumping up and chasing me down. That I'm going to miss.
<v Susan Watson>这是她作为我官方国防部联络员的工作——随时了解我在哪里，至少只要我决定告诉她的话。当我和吉娜下班后，纠缠在她位于东南部的舒适的两层楼房子里时，我喜欢叫她我的“操盘手”，然后赶在她能抓住我并对我进行盘问之前滚下床。 “保持原样，”她喊道，然后跳起来追赶我。那是我会怀念的。

00:44:48.450 --> 00:45:21.760
<v Susan Watson>Gina tells me to shut the door behind me. Then she says everything I think she's going to say, except not the way I think she's going to say it. Her voice is trembling. She's worried about me. Jesus, Gina. There you go overreacting again, I think to myself. I mean, the situation wasn't good. It was a shit show. But as military clusterfucks and hostile dust bowls go, it wasn't unusual. Really, it was just cross signals. US Special Forces had surrounded the guy's house, not knowing I was inside. I mean, how could they?
<v Susan Watson>吉娜让我把门关上。然后她说出所有我想她说的话，除了方式不是我想的那样。她的声音在颤抖。她在担心我。天哪，吉娜。我就在想，你又反应过度了。我的意思是，情况并不好。那是一团糟。但作为军事上的混乱和充满敌意的沙尘暴，这并不罕见。实际上，那只是信号交叉。美国特种部队包围了那家伙的房子，不知道我在里面。我的意思是，他们怎么可能知道？

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:38.630
<v Susan Watson>I want to tell Gina not to worry about me anymore. But then she'll want to know what that means. So instead I say what I've been planning to say since I put on my Ann Taylor blouse this morning. I wasn't there. It sucks that that happened, but I wasn't there.
<v Susan Watson>我想告诉吉娜不用再为我担心了。但那样她就会想知道这意味着什么。所以我说了我自从今天早上穿上安·泰勒衬衫以来就一直计划说的话。我不在那里。那是很不幸，但我不在那里。

00:45:38.630 --> 00:45:52.800
<v Susan Watson>Gina's quiet. My hand involuntarily reaches for my shirt collar, hoping to find the neck of the suit popping out. Then I remember I'm not wearing it. It's in the trunk of my car.
<v Susan Watson>吉娜沉默了。我的手不由自主地伸向衬衫领口，希望能找到弹出的套装领子。然后我才想起我没穿它。它在我的车后备箱里。

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:57.760
<v Susan Watson>"They airlifted him from Bamako to Walter Reed for observation," she says.
<v Susan Watson>“他们把他从巴马科空运到沃尔特·里德观察，”她说。

00:45:57.760 --> 00:46:00.140
<v Susan Watson>"What?" I snap.
<v Susan Watson>“什么？”我猛地说道。

00:46:00.140 --> 00:46:04.360
<v Susan Watson>"The civilian from Mali. He's at Walter Reed Hospital."
<v Susan Watson>“那个来自马里的平民。他在沃尔特·里德医院。”

00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:09.140
<v Susan Watson>"I thought he was dead." "I thought you said you weren't there."
<v Susan Watson>“我以为他死了。”“我以为你说你不在那里。”

00:46:09.140 --> 00:46:11.250
<v Susan Watson>I say nothing.
<v Susan Watson>我什么也没说。

00:46:11.250 --> 00:46:13.860
<v Susan Watson>"He's in a coma," she says.
<v Susan Watson>“他陷入了昏迷，”她说。

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:21.410
<v Susan Watson>My mind races back to that day. There was a lot of blood. I thought he was dead.
<v Susan Watson>我的思绪回到那一天。有很多血。我以为他死了。

00:46:21.410 --> 00:46:33.560
<v Susan Watson>"Kelly," barks Gina, as if saying my name will lasso me into this moment. "We've received reports that for days, the man had been telling friends and neighbors that he thought he was being haunted."
<v Susan Watson>“凯利，”吉娜厉声说道，仿佛叫我的名字就能把我拉回到这一刻。“我们收到报告说，几天来，那个人一直告诉朋友和邻居，他认为自己被鬼缠住了。”

00:46:33.560 --> 00:46:35.410
<v Susan Watson>"What are you talking about?" I ask.
<v Susan Watson>“你在说什么？”我问。

00:46:35.410 --> 00:46:47.420
<v Susan Watson>"Like a man or a woman that he could only see out of the corner of his eye when he wasn't really looking, watching him, following him. That's what he said. Like a ghost."
<v Susan Watson>“就像一个男人或一个女人，他只有在没怎么注意的时候才能从眼角余光看到，盯着他，跟着他。他是这么说的。就像一个鬼魂。”

00:46:47.420 --> 00:46:50.830
<v Susan Watson>"I wasn't there," I repeat.
<v Susan Watson>“我不在那里，”我重复道。

00:46:50.830 --> 00:47:19.280
<v Susan Watson>I see Gina's hand about to move toward me, but I'm already backing away. As I head for the elevator, I take small solace in the knowledge that when they come after me, they won't send her. I didn't shoot the guy in Mali. I was in the house with him, sure. But by then, I was wrapping up the investigation. He was no insurgent, not even a threat. Just a cagey dude waxing too political on Snapchat.
<v Susan Watson>我看到吉娜的手正要向我移动，但我已经在后退了。当我走向电梯时，我感到一丝安慰，知道当他们来追我时，他们不会派她来。我没有射杀那个在马里的家伙。当然，我确实和他在屋子里。但在那之前，我正要结束调查。他不是什么叛乱分子，甚至不是威胁。只是一个在 Snapchat 上大谈政治的谨慎家伙。

00:47:19.280 --> 00:47:41.880
<v Susan Watson>Besides, I was anxious as hell to get out of there. Honestly, I've been feeling off since I first touched down in Africa. When I was wearing the suit, I was jumpy. I was hearing things. I felt overstimulated and hyper aware. And I was starting to feel things, like other people's feelings when they passed too close to me in the street.
<v Susan Watson>此外，我非常渴望离开那里。老实说，自从我第一次降落在非洲，我就一直感觉不对劲。当我穿着套装时，我很紧张。我听到了一些声音。我觉得过度刺激并且极度警觉。我开始感觉到一些东西，比如当人们在街上离我太近时他们的感受。

00:47:41.880 --> 00:48:17.760
<v Susan Watson>The guy whose house I'd been snooping around, I could sense his fear. I didn't like how it felt, being inside other people like this, even if it was helpful. Like, when the special forces squad creeped on the house, nervous as hell, I felt them coming. It's like they were vibrating outside of their bodies through the walls, and I was an antenna. That's why I went outside-- to talk them down.
<v Susan Watson>我一直在监视那家伙的房子，我能感觉到他的恐惧。我不喜欢这种潜入别人内心深处的感觉，即使这很有帮助。比如，当特种部队小队溜向房子时，神经紧绷，我感觉到他们要来了。就像他们通过墙壁在体外震动，而我是一个天线。这就是我走出去的原因——去劝阻他们。

00:48:17.760 --> 00:48:44.550
<v Susan Watson>The suit's camouflage isn't perfect. Wearing it, I'm nearly impossible to spot in dark or busy areas. But if you stare right at it, it's like someone's smudged reality with a Photoshop brush, a moving distortion in space time. I walked calmly out the back door, but before I could pull the hood down and put my hands in the air, one of the special forces guys saw the smudge and let one go.
<v Susan Watson>套装的伪装并不完美。穿着它，在黑暗或忙碌的地方我几乎不可能被发现。但如果你直视它，就像有人用 Photoshop 笔刷涂抹了现实一样，是时空中的一个移动畸变。我冷静地从后门走出去，但在我能把兜帽拉下并把手举起来之前，特种部队的一个人看到了那个模糊的影子，开了一枪。

00:48:44.550 --> 00:49:27.700
<v Susan Watson>At the moment I heard the pop from his rifle, I felt a surge of, I guess, resistance that started in my chest and forced its way out through my pores. And when that wave reached the suit, it's like the suit radiated that force outward 50 times stronger. The bullet ricocheted off my shoulder through the door and into the house. I heard the civilian noncombatant scream and fall to the floor. I don't know what he said, but I know what he felt. It was as clear to me as if he'd whispered it right into my ear. Ghost.
<v Susan Watson>当我听到他步枪发出的砰声的那一刻，我感到胸中涌起一阵，我想，阻力，它强行穿过我的毛孔释放出来。当那股波浪到达套装时，感觉就像套装将那股力量向外辐射，强度增加了 50 倍。子弹从我的肩膀弹开，穿过门打进了屋子里。我听到那个平民非战斗人员尖叫着倒在地上。我不知道他说了什么，但我知道他感觉到了什么。这对我来说就像他在我耳边低语一样清晰。鬼魂。

00:49:27.700 --> 00:49:54.950
<v Susan Watson>Since Africa, I had been doing some research on the origins of the suit. As far back as the '90s, the military had been pouring money into researching melanin. It was known that certain birds used the chemical to change color. So it was a no brainer that defense would be looking at it as a way to camouflage troops. Some scientists also hypothesized that it was melanin that gave blood worms their incredible jaw strength. But I don't know shit about blood worms.
<v Susan Watson>从非洲回来后，我做了一些关于套装起源的研究。早在 90 年代，军方就一直在投入资金研究黑色素。已知某些鸟类利用这种化学物质改变颜色。所以国防部将其视为伪装部队的方法并不足为奇。一些科学家还假设是黑色素赋予了血虫不可思议的颚部力量。但我对血虫一无所知。

00:49:54.950 --> 00:50:24.520
<v Susan Watson>What I do know is that the suit was not designed to stop or repel bullets. It's not armor. Something is happening. The suit is changing, evolving. And I think it might have something to do with who's wearing it. I know this is going to sound crazy, but when I was a sophomore at Howard, this senior with an ankh around his neck used to follow me through the quad, calling me queen and preaching to me about the principles of comedic spirituality.
<v Susan Watson>我知道的是，这套衣服并不是为了阻挡或反弹子弹而设计的。它不是盔甲。有什么事情正在发生。套装正在改变，进化。我觉得这可能和谁穿着它有关。我知道这听起来很疯狂，但当我在霍华德大学读大二时，有个大四学长脖子上挂着十字章，常在校园里跟着我，叫我女王，并向我传授关于喜剧灵性的原则。

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:44.560
<v Susan Watson>I bet he would've had a field day with this suit. He'd say the concentrated melanin must be amplifying ancient African mysticism or whatever. He'd say the suit was helping me channel my ancestors. Hell, he'd probably paint a sankofa on the chest and teach himself how to fly. He was full of that kind of bullshit.
<v Susan Watson>我敢说他要是看到这套衣服肯定会如鱼得水。他会说浓缩的黑色素一定是在放大古老的非洲神秘主义之类的东西。他会说套装在帮我引导祖先。见鬼，他可能会在胸口画一个“Sankofa”符号，然后自学怎么飞。他满口都是那种废话。

00:50:44.560 --> 00:51:11.040
<v Susan Watson>Me, I'm thinking this suit never should have been made at all. That it's the result of cocky science assholes and soldiers hacking into natural power they don't understand and can't control. From the few very guarded conversations that I was able to have about this without setting off alarm bells over at the Pentagon, there is only one other person successfully deployed with the suit who ever reported side effects like mine.
<v Susan Watson>对我来说，我觉得这套衣服压根就不应该被制造出来。它是那些自大的科学混蛋和士兵黑进了他们根本不懂也无法控制的自然力量的产物。从我能就此事进行的一些非常谨慎的对话来看，在五角大楼那边没有敲响警钟的情况下，只有另外一个人成功部署并穿着这套衣服，且报告过像我这样的副作用。

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:38.340
<v Susan Watson>That brother, a tough ex-Marine from South Philly, is currently doodling on the wall at a psychiatric hospital somewhere in New Mexico. The hospital staff won't go near him. They say he can tell you what you had for breakfast or who you're fucking just by looking at you. That's not going to be me, stuck in some psych ward somewhere, David Blaine to a bunch of orderlies. That's why I'm running.
<v Susan Watson>那个兄弟，一个来自费城南部、强硬的前海军陆战队队员，目前正被关在新墨西哥州某处的精神病院里，在那儿在墙上乱涂乱画。医院工作人员都不敢靠近他。他们说他只要看你一眼，就能告诉你你早饭吃了什么或者你在跟谁上床。我不打算变成那样，被困在某个精神病房里，成为一群护理员眼里的“大卫·布莱恩”。这就是我为什么要逃的原因。

00:51:38.340 --> 00:51:59.510
<v Susan Watson>After Mali, I knew I needed some time alone with the suit, to figure out exactly what was going on, what it was doing to me. I figured I'd do some beta testing of my own. I'd go off the grid, head west, maybe even make my way to New Mexico or San Diego, where a lot of the original melanin research was done.
<v Susan Watson>马里事件后，我知道我需要一些时间和这套衣服独处，好弄清楚到底发生了什么，它对我做了什么。我想我可以做一些自己的 Beta 测试。我要脱离系统，向西进发，甚至可能前往新墨西哥州或圣地亚哥，那里做了很多最初的黑色素研究。

00:51:59.510 --> 00:52:26.280
<v Susan Watson>No one's supposed to know about the suits, not foreign governments. Folks in the White House don't even know. So I knew that if I took off with it, someone would come looking to take it back. Until this morning, I was pretty confident I could stay gone. But now, I have another item on my to do list. And I have to take care of it before I can leave.
<v Susan Watson>没人应该知道这套衣服的存在，包括外国政府。白宫里的人甚至不知道。所以我知道，如果我带着它逃走，肯定会有人来找它。直到今天早上，我都很确信我能一直逃下去。但现在，我的待办事项里又多了一项。在我离开之前，我必须处理掉它。

00:52:26.280 --> 00:53:05.850
<v Susan Watson>I meant what I said to Gina about this sort of eye witness who was currently convalescing in Bethesda. It sucked that he caught wind of me. It sucked that he caught a bullet that was meant for me. Hell, it sucked that he'd gotten caught up in all this at all. But coma or no coma, he was proof-- literally living proof-- that I'd been at the center of that mess in Mali. And everything I'd learned over the years about how to do dirt and get away clean told me that this was simply not something I could let go. He was a loose end that needed to be tied off.
<v Susan Watson>我对吉娜说的关于那个目前在贝塞斯达疗养的目击者的事是真心的。他撞见我真是倒霉。他中了一颗本来是给我的子弹真是倒霉。见鬼，他卷进这一切真是倒霉。但不管是昏迷还是清醒，他都是证据——货真价实的活证据——证明我当时就在马里那场混乱的中心。而我多年来学到的关于如何做脏活并全身而退的一切都告诉我，这根本不是我能轻易放过的事情。他是一个需要清理掉的松散环节。

00:53:05.850 --> 00:53:41.960
<v Susan Watson>Slipping into a secure military hospital sounds difficult, but not when you have a top secret cloaking device or when everyone inside already thinks you're a nurse. I'm on the right floor in the correct ward before I even have to put the suit on. I stare at it for a minute. It's an impenetrable impossible matte black. But at the same time, if you gaze into the fabric, it's every color in the spectrum. It feels good to put it back on. I realize as it bonds to my skin that I've been missing it. I feel stronger, like twice myself.
<v Susan Watson>潜入一所戒备森严的军事医院听起来很难，但当你有一个绝密的隐身装置，或者医院里的每个人都以为你是护士时，就不难了。我甚至不需要穿上套装就到了那个病房的正确楼层。我盯着它看了一会儿。它是无法穿透的、不可思议的哑光黑。但与此同时，如果你凝视布料，它是光谱中的每一种颜色。重新穿上它感觉真好。当它粘合到我的皮肤上时，我意识到我一直都在想念它。我感到更强大了，就像是两倍的自己。

00:53:41.960 --> 00:54:09.260
<v Susan Watson>I shake out of this Bilbo Baggins moment and exit the bathroom stall. I sneak easily around the two schlubs who got stuck guarding a coma patient and enter the Mali guy's room. He's alone in his bed, breathing slowly through a tube. Machines chirp all around him. I approach the bed and check the name on the chart. John Doe. Of course. Luckily, I'm wearing psychic pajamas.
<v Susan Watson>我从这种“比尔博·巴金斯”的时刻中清醒过来，离开了厕所隔间。我轻松地避开了那两个被困住去守着昏迷病人的蠢货，进入了那个马里人的房间。他独自躺在床上，通过管子缓慢地呼吸着。机器在他周围发出叽叽喳喳的声音。我走近床边，查看病历上的名字。无名氏。当然。幸运的是，我穿着通灵睡衣。

00:54:09.260 --> 00:54:30.730
<v Susan Watson>I lean in and put my hand over his. And whoa. This is new. His memories come rushing at me. It's like his mind pours up through the sleeves of the suit and into mine. It takes me a second to slow it down. His name is Oumar.
<v Susan Watson>我俯下身，把手放在他的手上。哇。这很新鲜。他的记忆冲向我。就像他的思维顺着套装的袖子涌进了我的大脑。我花了一秒钟才让它慢下来。他的名字叫乌马尔。

00:54:30.730 --> 00:55:04.220
<v Susan Watson>Wow. I really underestimated this guy. I flip through his recent past like a carousel slide show. The entire time I'd been watching Oumar in Mali, he'd been watching me or trying. He'd actually caught a few glimpses of me at my sloppiest, circling around behind him to see who he was texting, sliding into his room as he drifted off to sleep. He'd been looking right at me as the bullet bounced off me and struck his skull. He's going to recover from that, by the way. His vitals are good.
<v Susan Watson>哇。我真的低估了这个家伙。我像看旋转木马幻灯片一样翻阅他的近期往事。我盯着乌马尔在马里的整个期间，他一直在盯着我，或者尝试盯着我。他确实在我最粗心的时候瞥见了我几次，他在我绕到他身后看他在给谁发短信，在我溜进他房间时他正陷入沉睡时。当子弹从我身上弹开打中他的头骨时，他正正地看着我。顺便说一句，他会康复的。他的生命体征很好。

00:55:04.220 --> 00:55:32.120
<v Susan Watson>I find myself thinking, maybe I don't have to do what I came here to do after all. It's not like he actually saw my face. So what if he wakes up and tells a few more people he saw a ghost? What's the harm in that? The thought surprises me. I'm used to cleaning up after myself. But I'm also relieved. I back away from the hospital bed, thinking if I leave now, I'll still have a pretty good head start on whatever spooks are chasing me down.
<v Susan Watson>我发现自己在想，也许我不需要做我来这里做的事了。毕竟他并没有真的看到我的脸。那又怎样？如果他醒过来，告诉更多人他看到了鬼魂？那又有什么害处呢？这个想法让我自己都感到惊讶。我习惯了清理自己的后事。但我也感到释然。我后退离开医院病床，想如果我现在离开，我在任何追捕我的鬼魂面前都还能有相当大的领先优势。

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:59.760
<v Susan Watson>That's when it occurs to me. I can't just leave him here. The people who are coming after me have way more to lose than I do. If they're ready to kill me for stealing the suit, they'll take Oumar out just for knowing about it. I'm still holding his hand. I squeeze his fingers, scan his dark features, and then I realized, I can't let that happen. I have to protect him.
<v Susan Watson>就在这时，我意识到了一件事。我不能就这样把他留在这里。那些来追我的人比我损失的东西要多得多。如果他们为了夺回套装而准备杀我，他们也会仅仅因为乌马尔知道这件事而把他干掉。我仍然握着他的手。我紧紧抓着他的手指，看着他黑色的五官，然后我意识到，我不能让那种事发生。我必须保护他。

00:55:59.760 --> 00:56:33.330
<v Susan Watson>The thought really freaks me out. It seems to come from somewhere deeper inside me than me, pulled up to the surface by-- by-- oh, shit. It's the suit. I know it. First, it has me reading minds, and now I'm supposed to care about this guy. Not convenient. Not cool. I could die right here today fighting for some African dude I don't even know, all because of a double extra strength dose of melanin.
<v Susan Watson>这个想法真的把我吓坏了。它似乎来自我内心深处，被——被——噢，见鬼。是这套衣服。我知道是它。首先，它让我读心，现在我又必须关心这个家伙。太不方便了。太不酷了。我可能今天就要死在这里，为某个我不认识的非洲人而战，全是因为这超强剂量的黑色素。

00:56:33.330 --> 00:57:03.590
<v Susan Watson>I get a text from Gina that reads simply, "as you were." I know she means they're coming, and I should run. I don't. I move to stand between Oumar's bed and the door. I've got no weapon, and my hand-to-hand combat training is rusty as all hell. Pray the ancestors can help me with that. I take off my earrings.
<v Susan Watson>我收到吉娜的一条短信，内容仅仅是：“保持原样。”我知道她是说他们来了，我该跑了。我没跑。我走到乌马尔的床和门之间。我没有武器，我的肉搏训练已经生锈透了。祈求祖先能在这一点上帮我吧。我摘下耳环。

00:57:03.590 --> 00:57:14.290
